 <h1>Seven minimal experiments · 2026-10-30</h1>
 <p class=note>Main data: EfficientNet-B0 raw vectors (Git, SHA-256 checked; all 1,641 image_ids and splits matched row by row against
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@ Labels: organ = BioImages organ_category (single label); scale = BioImages + Gem
 <p><span class='b v'>Verified</span> <span class='b e'>Exploratory demo</span> <span class='b x'>Not done / blocked</span> <span class='b r'>Recovered earlier result (not recomputed)</span></p>
 <p class=note><b>Verified</b> = fixed protocol, held-out data or resampling control, numbers reproducible from the scripts.
 <b>Exploratory demo</b> = individual images and readings not validated against independent labels.</p>
-<nav><a href=#e1>1</a><a href=#e2>2</a><a href=#e3>3</a><a href=#e4>4</a><a href=#e5>5</a><a href=#e6>6</a><a href=#e7>7</a><a href=#e8>appendix</a></nav><section id=e1><h2>1. NSC: performance vs number of retained coordinates</h2><div><span class='b v'>Verified</span> <span class='b e'>Exploratory demo</span></div><dl><dt>Question</dt><dd>Can a few PCA coordinates keep organ separation, or reproduce the existing cluster grouping?</dd><dt>What was actually run</dt><dd>EfficientNet-B0 raw vectors → PCA-64 (fit on train only, whitened). Own NSC implementation (Tibshirani 2002); 33-point Δ grid; Δ chosen by 5-fold GroupKFold on train (groups = individual plant) with the one-standard-error rule; test evaluated once. Task A: external organ label (BioImages organ_category, 7 classes). Task B: <b>grouping-approximation task</b>, where the target is the existing EfficientNet K=20 cluster ID. That target comes from the same vectors, so the task is circular.</dd><dt>Result</dt><dd><div class=tw><table><tr><th>task</th><th>n_train</th><th>n_test</th><th>classes</th><th>delta_1se</th><th>coords_1se</th><th>coords_per_class_1se</th><th>test_F1_delta0</th><th>test_F1_1se</th><th>ratio</th></tr><tr><td>organ (external label)</td><td>1169</td><td>211</td><td>7</td><td>1.500</td><td>22</td><td>7.857</td><td>0.692</td><td>0.672</td><td>0.971</td></tr><tr><td>grouping approximation: own K=20 cluster ID</td><td>1181</td><td>211</td><td>20</td><td>1.250</td><td>61</td><td>21.850</td><td>0.865</td><td>0.853</td><td>0.986</td></tr></table></div><img src='results/exp1_nsc_curve.png' alt='NSC curve'></dd><dt>What it supports</dt><dd>Organ: at the 1-SE Δ=1.5 only 22/64 coordinates remain (7.9 per class on average). Test macro-F1 is 0.672 vs 0.692 at Δ=0 (97% retained). The grouping-approximation task barely sparsifies (61/64): K-means groups are spread over most components. <b>These coordinates are PCA components, not biological features.</b> Single split, single model; cross-fold stability (Jaccard) not done.</dd></dl></section><section id=e2><h2>2. Information kept by PCA + composition and enrichment of existing clusters</h2><div><span class='b v'>Verified</span></div><dl><dt>Question</dt><dd>How much does dimensionality reduction lose? What organ / scale / species mix does each existing K=20 cluster have?</dd><dt>What was actually run</dt><dd>PCA retention: see the table in item 6 (EfficientNet, fit on train / reported on val). Enrichment: existing K=20 cluster IDs of BioCLIP, DINOv3 and EfficientNet (all 1,641 images) × organ / shot scale. Reported: count, base rate, fold enrichment (FE), 95% CI from a bootstrap over <b>individual plants</b> (500 resamples), species-adjusted FE (observed / expected from the cluster's species mix), and top-species share. <b>No p-values</b>: images are nested in 465 individuals and 63 species, so the independence assumption of Fisher / chi-square tests fails.</dd><dt>Result</dt><dd>PCA (EfficientNet, val): 2-D keeps 16.5% of variance, 64-D 68.4%, 256-D 89.5%.<div class=tw><table><tr><th>representation/label</th><th>clusters</th><th>clusters_dominant_share_ge_0_6</th><th>clusters_fe_ci_lo_gt_1_5</th><th>clusters_species_adjusted_fe_gt_1_5</th><th>median_top_species_share</th><th>clusters_lt5_individuals</th></tr><tr><td>bioclip/organ</td><td>20.000</td><td>2.000</td><td>4.000</td><td>5.000</td><td>0.327</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip/shot_scale</td><td>20.000</td><td>8.000</td><td>2.000</td><td>2.000</td><td>0.327</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3/organ</td><td>20.000</td><td>17.000</td><td>19.000</td><td>19.000</td><td>0.115</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3/shot_scale</td><td>20.000</td><td>13.000</td><td>6.000</td><td>9.000</td><td>0.115</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>eff/organ</td><td>20.000</td><td>17.000</td><td>19.000</td><td>20.000</td><td>0.088</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>eff/shot_scale</td><td>20.000</td><td>14.000</td><td>8.000</td><td>8.000</td><td>0.088</td><td>0.000</td></tr></table></div><p>Top 3 organ clusters per model by species-adjusted FE:</p><div class=tw><table><tr><th>representation</th><th>cluster</th><th>label</th><th>n_images</th><th>cluster_share</th><th>fold_enrichment</th><th>fe_ci_lo</th><th>fe_ci_hi</th><th>species_adjusted_fe</th><th>n_individuals</th><th>n_species</th><th>top_species_share</th></tr><tr><td>eff</td><td>5</td><td>bark</td><td>86</td><td>1.000</td><td>9.596</td><td>8.360</td><td>11.184</td><td>7.975</td><td>69</td><td>39</td><td>0.128</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>4</td><td>bark</td><td>81</td><td>1.000</td><td>9.596</td><td>8.360</td><td>11.184</td><td>7.895</td><td>63</td><td>32</td><td>0.136</td></tr><tr><td>eff</td><td>19</td><td>fruit</td><td>23</td><td>1.000</td><td>7.852</td><td>6.729</td><td>9.726</td><td>6.544</td><td>12</td><td>5</td><td>0.783</td></tr><tr><td>eff</td><td>18</td><td>bark</td><td>67</td><td>0.910</td><td>8.737</td><td>7.488</td><td>10.143</td><td>6.446</td><td>58</td><td>38</td><td>0.075</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>1</td><td>twig</td><td>79</td><td>0.886</td><td>7.234</td><td>6.103</td><td>8.528</td><td>6.395</td><td>61</td><td>42</td><td>0.076</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>12</td><td>bark</td><td>80</td><td>0.900</td><td>8.637</td><td>7.445</td><td>9.985</td><td>6.371</td><td>68</td><td>43</td><td>0.087</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>11</td><td>whole tree</td><td>121</td><td>0.868</td><td>4.464</td><td>4.022</td><td>4.995</td><td>3.074</td><td>78</td><td>26</td><td>0.124</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>17</td><td>whole tree</td><td>122</td><td>0.631</td><td>3.247</td><td>2.737</td><td>3.777</td><td>2.705</td><td>85</td><td>33</td><td>0.098</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>3</td><td>other</td><td>200</td><td>0.030</td><td>3.282</td><td>0.919</td><td>7.380</td><td>2.187</td><td>109</td><td>54</td><td>0.090</td></tr></table></div><img src='results/exp2_organ_composition.png' alt='organ composition'></dd><dt>What it supports</dt><dd>Descriptive effects. In DINOv3 / EfficientNet, 19–20 of 20 clusters have a dominant organ with FE CI lower bound &gt;1.5, still &gt;1.5 after adjusting for species mix, and the median top-species share is only 9–12%. These organ clusters are not single-species clusters. BioCLIP has 4–5 such clusters out of 20, with a median top-species share of 33%, so it clusters by species. This matches the NMI in README §2 and now also survives individual resampling and species adjustment. Labels: single-label organ_category; scale labels are provisional.</dd></dl></section><section id=e3><h2>3. Activation mapping (Grad-CAM + occlusion control)</h2><div><span class='b v'>Verified</span> <span class='b e'>Exploratory demo</span></div><dl><dt>Question</dt><dd>When EfficientNet pushes an image towards a cluster, which image regions does it rely on? Is the map faithful to the score?</dd><dt>What was actually run</dt><dd>EfficientNet-B0 weights and all 1,641 original images are available, so this is a real computation (CPU, fp32). Check first: re-embedding 40 images gives median cosine 0.9994 to the stored vectors (min 0.9976). Target score = cosine between the image embedding and its K=20 cluster centroid. Per cluster, 2 core + 2 boundary images by distance to the centroid (seed 42, not hand-picked; 80 images). Occlusion control: replace the top 12/49 Grad-CAM cells with mean grey vs 10 random sets of 12 cells. Sanity check: re-initialising the last feature block should change the map. <b>BioCLIP / DINOv3 not run</b>: this needs their model weights and a GPU-scale run, which this round did not do (their raw vectors were recovered later, see appendix).</dd><dt>Result</dt><dd>Fraction of cases where Grad-CAM occlusion drops the score more than random occlusion: 88% (core 85%, boundary 90%). Median drop 0.065 vs random 0.009. Spearman between the original map and the map after randomising the last block: median 0.15 (the map depends on the weights; check passed).<img src='results/exp3_gradcam_cases.png' alt='Grad-CAM cases'><p>The 8 cases above follow a fixed rule (first core and first boundary image of the 4 largest clusters). In the c0 core case the hot area is background at the top of the image, not the inflorescence.</p></dd><dt>What it supports</dt><dd>The maps are <b>faithful</b> to the target score (occlusion control). Whether they fall <b>on the organ is not tested</b>: there are no organ masks, so no claim such as "the cluster looks at the leaf, not the background" is supported. Individual maps are exploratory.</dd></dl></section><section id=e4><h2>4. Soft clustering / K</h2><div><span class='b v'>Verified</span> <span class='b e'>Exploratory demo</span> <span class='b x'>Not done / blocked</span></div><dl><dt>Question</dt><dd>Which K? Do some images really sit between two clusters, and does that ambiguity mean anything?</dd><dt>What was actually run</dt><dd>Reused the existing K=10/20/50 × 10 seeds results (species/organ NMI, between-seed ARI). New: diagonal GMM (K=20) on EfficientNet PCA-32, 5 seeds; per image the maximum membership and membership entropy; BIC over K∈{5,10,20,30,50}; whether entropy predicts probe errors on test, and mid-range scale.</dd><dt>Result</dt><dd><div class=tw><table><tr><th>representation</th><th>k</th><th>seeds</th><th>species_nmi_mean</th><th>organ_nmi_mean</th><th>stability_pairwise_ari_mean</th></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>0.466</td><td>0.133</td><td>0.597</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>20</td><td>10</td><td>0.597</td><td>0.146</td><td>0.519</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip</td><td>50</td><td>10</td><td>0.734</td><td>0.196</td><td>0.581</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>0.217</td><td>0.503</td><td>0.588</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>20</td><td>10</td><td>0.322</td><td>0.498</td><td>0.594</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>50</td><td>10</td><td>0.457</td><td>0.490</td><td>0.563</td></tr><tr><td>efficientnet_b0</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>0.128</td><td>0.535</td><td>0.709</td></tr><tr><td>efficientnet_b0</td><td>20</td><td>10</td><td>0.212</td><td>0.498</td><td>0.544</td></tr><tr><td>efficientnet_b0</td><td>50</td><td>10</td><td>0.327</td><td>0.456</td><td>0.440</td></tr></table></div><p>GMM BIC: K=5: -99,358, K=10: -102,657, K=20: -105,956, K=30: -106,382, K=50: -103,058 (lowest at K=30; flattens between K=20 and 30). Between-seed label ARI 0.66, entropy-rank Spearman 0.76; ARI vs existing K-means 0.54. Only 1.3% of images have maximum membership &lt;0.6. AUROC of entropy for probe errors: <b>0.51</b> (211 test images, 165 wrong); for mid-range scale: 0.64.</p><p>The 8 highest-entropy real images (p1/p2 = probabilities of the top two components; in brackets, that component's dominant organ):</p><div class=strip><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/trhcol2lf10939.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/trhcol2lf10939.jpg' alt='baskauf/10939'></a><figcaption>baskauf/10939<br>Rhus copallinum<br>leaf·close-up<br>p1=0.35(leaf 41%) p2=0.33(leaf 77%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tceoc2-fr37494.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tceoc2-fr37494.jpg' alt='baskauf/37494'></a><figcaption>baskauf/37494<br>Cephalanthus occidentalis<br>fruit·close-up<br>p1=0.60(inflorescence 77%) p2=0.19(inflorescence 58%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tilve--twfemale-buds35376.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tilve--twfemale-buds35376.jpg' alt='baskauf/35376'></a><figcaption>baskauf/35376<br>Ilex verticillata<br>twig·close-up<br>p1=0.64(inflorescence 77%) p2=0.17(leaf 77%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tacne2-flinflor31644.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tacne2-flinflor31644.jpg' alt='baskauf/31644'></a><figcaption>baskauf/31644<br>Acer negundo<br>inflorescence·close-up<br>p1=0.48(inflorescence 58%) p2=0.27(inflorescence 77%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tzaam--flfemale-inflor51814.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tzaam--flfemale-inflor51814.jpg' alt='baskauf/51814'></a><figcaption>baskauf/51814<br>Zanthoxylum americanum<br>inflorescence·close-up<br>p1=0.46(twig 89%) p2=0.32(leaf 41%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/ttsca--cofemale-devel10805.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/ttsca--cofemale-devel10805.jpg' alt='baskauf/10805'></a><figcaption>baskauf/10805<br>Tsuga canadensis<br>cone·close-up<br>p1=0.49(leaf 41%) p2=0.26(leaf 77%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcade12flinflor35249.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcade12flinflor35249.jpg' alt='baskauf/35249'></a><figcaption>baskauf/35249<br>Castanea dentata<br>inflorescence·close-up<br>p1=0.48(leaf 41%) p2=0.38(inflorescence 77%)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tpist--comale34219.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tpist--comale34219.jpg' alt='baskauf/34219'></a><figcaption>baskauf/34219<br>Pinus strobus<br>cone·close-up<br>p1=0.66(inflorescence 77%) p2=0.17(inflorescence 58%)</figcaption></figure></div></dd><dt>What it supports</dt><dd>In 32-D the diagonal GMM is very hard: ambiguous images are rare. Entropy does not predict classification errors (0.51 ≈ chance) and carries a weak signal for mid-range scale (0.64). <b>Soft probability is not multi-organ evidence.</b> Multi-organ AUROC cannot be computed: no multi-label organ labels are in Git (Gemma multi-label / Human Gold not exported). The example images are exploratory.</dd></dl></section><section id=e5><h2>5. AE recovery + real images at both ends of PCA axes</h2><div><span class='b r'>Recovered earlier result (not recomputed)</span> <span class='b v'>Verified</span> <span class='b e'>Exploratory demo</span></div><dl><dt>Question</dt><dd>Is a nonlinear AE better than PCA? What do the main PCA directions correspond to?</dd><dt>What was actually run</dt><dd>Recovered the AE vs PCA comparison from the Experiment 6 table on the hosted page scale/meeting/ (raw HTML kept in recovered_site/). One added check: a linear AE (32-D, 3,000 epochs) should match PCA-32. Axis reading: EfficientNet PCA (fit on train) PC1–5, Spearman with scale (distant=0 → close=2), each organ one-vs-rest, and thumbnail brightness; 6 real images at each end. No architecture search.</dd><dt>Result</dt><dd><div class=tw><table><tr><th>model</th><th>method</th><th>test_mse</th><th>knn15_retention</th></tr><tr><td>bioclip25</td><td>PCA</td><td>0.000284</td><td>0.667299</td></tr><tr><td>bioclip25</td><td>Autoencoder</td><td>0.000280</td><td>0.616746</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>PCA</td><td>0.000498</td><td>0.766509</td></tr><tr><td>dinov3</td><td>Autoencoder</td><td>0.000491</td><td>0.745656</td></tr><tr><td>efficientnet_b0</td><td>PCA</td><td>0.000296</td><td>0.729542</td></tr><tr><td>efficientnet_b0</td><td>Autoencoder</td><td>0.000301</td><td>0.721327</td></tr></table></div><p>Linear-AE check: PCA-32 test MSE 0.0002961, linear AE 0.0002971 (ratio 1.003). This PCA-32 value also equals the hosted EfficientNet PCA value 0.000296, so code and data line up.</p><div class=tw><table><tr><th>pc</th><th>var</th><th>rho_scale_close</th><th>rho_brightness</th><th>rho_leaf</th><th>rho_whole tree</th><th>rho_inflorescence</th><th>rho_fruit</th><th>rho_twig</th><th>rho_bark</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.10</td><td>-0.65</td><td>0.33</td><td>-0.56</td><td>0.62</td><td>-0.10</td><td>-0.04</td><td>-0.17</td><td>0.34</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.06</td><td>-0.44</td><td>0.11</td><td>0.32</td><td>0.45</td><td>0.01</td><td>-0.10</td><td>-0.37</td><td>-0.51</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.05</td><td>-0.04</td><td>0.14</td><td>0.55</td><td>0.03</td><td>-0.43</td><td>-0.30</td><td>-0.26</td><td>0.38</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.03</td><td>-0.10</td><td>0.04</td><td>-0.04</td><td>-0.08</td><td>0.05</td><td>-0.03</td><td>0.07</td><td>0.09</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.03</td><td>-0.15</td><td>-0.13</td><td>0.04</td><td>0.17</td><td>-0.41</td><td>0.01</td><td>0.40</td><td>-0.21</td></tr></table></div><h4>PC1 low end</h4><div class=strip><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tzaam--lfpetiole-lower35728.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tzaam--lfpetiole-lower35728.jpg' alt='baskauf/35728'></a><figcaption>baskauf/35728<br>Zanthoxylum americanum<br>leaf · close-up</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tpotr5-lfmargin-uplow14027.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tpotr5-lfmargin-uplow14027.jpg' alt='baskauf/14027'></a><figcaption>baskauf/14027<br>Populus tremuloides<br>leaf · close-up</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcoob9-lfmargin-uplow54683.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcoob9-lfmargin-uplow54683.jpg' alt='baskauf/54683'></a><figcaption>baskauf/54683<br>Cornus amomum<br>leaf · close-up</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/taial--lfgland15196.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/taial--lfgland15196.jpg' alt='baskauf/15196'></a><figcaption>baskauf/15196<br>Ailanthus altissima<br>leaf · close-up</figcaption></figure><figure><a href='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcaco15lfmargin-uplow52430.jpg' target=_blank><img loading=lazy src='https://bioimages.vanderbilt.edu/tn/baskauf/tcaco15lfmargin-uplo